        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Benchtop Planer Picks",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-benchtop-planer/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which benchtop planer should a home woodworker buy first?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most home shops, the DeWalt DW735X is the strongest all-round choice, with two feed speeds, 13-inch capacity and included tables. If noise is a concern, the Makita 2012NB is much quieter. If you work with figured or reclaimed wood and want a helical head on a budget, the WEN PL1326 is the value pick."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will a thickness planer fix a cupped or twisted board?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. Roller pressure holds the board flat as it passes the cutterhead, and it springs back once released, leaving a thinner board with the same warp. You need a flat reference face first, made on a jointer, with a planer sled, or by hand. Then the planer makes the opposite face parallel and brings the board to thickness."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are helical cutterheads worth the extra cost?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For figured, curly or interlocked grain, and for reclaimed lumber, yes. Helical heads generally reduce tear-out, run quieter, and let you rotate a single damaged insert instead of replacing a knife set. For straight-grained wood like pine, poplar and oak, a sharp straight-knife head works well and costs less to buy and maintain."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I stop snipe on my planer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Level the infeed and outfeed tables with the bed, support long boards with stands, lift the board ends slightly as they enter and exit, feed boards end to end, and use the carriage lock if your planer has one. The most reliable fix is to leave parts a few inches long and cut the sniped ends off after planing."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How deep should each planer pass be?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most woodworkers take passes of about 1/32 to 1/16 inch, especially on wide boards and near final thickness. Manufacturers list maximum depths, often 1/8 inch on narrow boards, but heavy cuts strain the motor and increase tear-out and snipe. Several light passes usually produce a better surface in about the same total time."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use a shop vac for planer dust collection?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For occasional use, a shop vac with a 2.5-inch hose and a cyclone separator can keep up with a benchtop planer. Without a separator, the vac filter clogs quickly and the tank fills fast. For regular milling sessions, a dust collector with a 4-inch hose handles the volume of chips much better and keeps the planer from jamming."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need a jointer if I own a planer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not strictly. If you buy lumber that is already surfaced and reasonably flat, a planer alone brings it to thickness. For rough lumber, you need a way to create a flat face and straight edge. A jointer is the fastest way, but a planer sled for faces and a table saw jig for edges work well for occasional milling."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "All Metal Brake" },
