          <p>
            The payback comes from lumber prices. Rough-sawn hardwood from a local mill or lumberyard is commonly much cheaper per board foot than surfaced boards from a home center, and you get to choose thickness, grain and width. Anyone who builds a few pieces of furniture per year can recover the cost of a planer within a few projects.
          </p>

          <p>
            A planer is not worth buying if you build occasional small projects from surfaced pine and plywood. In that case, buy lumber at the thickness you need. If you want to build furniture from hardwood, reuse barn boards or old flooring, or work with figured wood, a planer is one of the most valuable tools in the shop. Pair it with a good vac or collector from our <a href="/best-shop-vac/">shop vac picks</a> before the first board goes through.
          </p>
