        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting It to Flatten a Warped Board</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A cupped or twisted board gets squashed flat by the rollers while the cutterhead cuts it, and the board springs back as it leaves. The result is a thinner, still-warped board. Create a flat reference face first with a jointer, a planer sled or a hand plane, then use the planer to make the other face parallel.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Taking Heavy Passes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Removing the maximum listed depth on every pass bogs down the motor, increases tear-out and snipe, and wears knives faster. Light passes of 1/32 to 1/16 inch produce better surfaces and are rarely slower overall. Use the depth gauge if your planer has one and save heavier cuts for narrow boards.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cutting Parts to Final Length Before Planing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Snipe concentrates at the ends of boards. If parts are already at final length, the snipe ends up in the finished piece. Leave parts a few inches long, plane them, then trim the ends. It wastes a small amount of lumber and avoids sanding or scraping out every sniped end.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Planing Reclaimed Wood Without Checking for Metal</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Old nails, staples, screws and embedded grit can chip knives, damage helical inserts and throw fragments. Scan reclaimed lumber with a metal detector, remove anything you find, and brush off dirt before planing. Check again after each pass, because buried metal can surface as material comes off.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running Without Dust Collection</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Chips that are not removed get pressed back into the board as dents, clog the chute and spread across the shop. Connect a dust collector or a wide-hose shop vac with a cyclone separator before you start. It improves surface quality, makes cleanup faster and keeps the air cleaner.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which benchtop planer should a home woodworker buy first?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most home shops, the DeWalt DW735X is the strongest all-round choice, with two feed speeds, 13-inch capacity and included tables. If noise is a concern, the Makita 2012NB is much quieter. If you work with figured or reclaimed wood and want a helical head on a budget, the WEN PL1326 is the value pick.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a thickness planer fix a cupped or twisted board?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Roller pressure holds the board flat as it passes the cutterhead, and it springs back once released, leaving a thinner board with the same warp. You need a flat reference face first, made on a jointer, with a planer sled, or by hand. Then the planer makes the opposite face parallel and brings the board to thickness.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are helical cutterheads worth the extra cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For figured, curly or interlocked grain, and for reclaimed lumber, yes. Helical heads generally reduce tear-out, run quieter, and let you rotate a single damaged insert instead of replacing a knife set. For straight-grained wood like pine, poplar and oak, a sharp straight-knife head works well and costs less to buy and maintain.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I stop snipe on my planer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Level the infeed and outfeed tables with the bed, support long boards with stands, lift the board ends slightly as they enter and exit, feed boards end to end, and use the carriage lock if your planer has one. The most reliable fix is to leave parts a few inches long and cut the sniped ends off after planing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How deep should each planer pass be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most woodworkers take passes of about 1/32 to 1/16 inch, especially on wide boards and near final thickness. Manufacturers list maximum depths, often 1/8 inch on narrow boards, but heavy cuts strain the motor and increase tear-out and snipe. Several light passes usually produce a better surface in about the same total time.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a shop vac for planer dust collection?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For occasional use, a shop vac with a 2.5-inch hose and a cyclone separator can keep up with a benchtop planer. Without a separator, the vac filter clogs quickly and the tank fills fast. For regular milling sessions, a dust collector with a 4-inch hose handles the volume of chips much better and keeps the planer from jamming.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a jointer if I own a planer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not strictly. If you buy lumber that is already surfaced and reasonably flat, a planer alone brings it to thickness. For rough lumber, you need a way to create a flat face and straight edge. A jointer is the fastest way, but a planer sled for faces and a table saw jig for edges work well for occasional milling.
              </p>
